
{"id":"1fc3f5ad-d332-4792-9340-3c28340ee7d7","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025123,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) per the spec. The case payload has \"items\" (list of strings) and \"required_failure_items\" (list of strings). The task: apply chunk removal and granularity refinement. We start with chunk granularity 2, try chunks left to right, accept removal only if required failure items remain (i.e., after removal, the required failure items are still all present?). Actually \"accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" Means: when we remove a chunk, we check that all required_failure_items are still in the reduced items after removal. If yes, we accept the removal and restart with slightly coarser granularity (increase granularity?). The spec: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So after acceptance, we increase granularity? But granularity is chunk size. Starting at 2, after acceptance we increase? Then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So if no removal at current granularity succeeds, we double the granularity (chunk size). And stop when granularity becomes 1 (singleton). That seems contradictory: start at 2, if removal succeeds, we coarsen (increase) granularity? Possibly \"slightly coarser\" means larger chunk size? But then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" also increases granularity. Hmm maybe \"slightly coarser\" means go back to a finer granularity? The phrasing: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" Could mean we reset granularity to a coarser (larger) value? Or maybe \"coarser\" means smaller granularity? Typically coarser means larger chunks. I need to interpret correctly. Let's re-read: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if