e task objective: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" And \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So it's like a minimization: we want to reduce the items as much as possible while keeping the required failure items. The algorithm is similar to delta debugging but with specific granularity changes. The spec says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That could mean after a successful removal, we increase the chunk size (coarser) for the next pass, to try to remove larger contiguous blocks? But then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" also increases granularity. That seems redundant. Maybe \"coarser\" means finer? No.\n\nAlternatively, it might be a typo or specific interpretation: \"slightly coarser\" might mean go back to the previous (coarser) granularity, i.e., if we were at 2 and succeeded, we go to 1? But 1 is finer, not coarser. Or maybe it's called \"coarser\" relative to the current? Actually, if we start at 2 (fine), after success we go to 3 (coarser), then if no removal at 3, we double to 6, etc., and stop at singleton (1) when we try to go beyond? But we never go below 2. So stop at singleton might mean when we try to go to 1, but we never do because we only increase.\n\nLet's look at the public fixtures: they have 10 items, required failure items are two specific strings. The expected output? We don't have expected output, but we need to implement algorithm that returns reduced_items, attempts, and one_minimal boolean. The spec says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So we need to compute whether the final reduced items is minimal (i.e., cannot remove any single item while keeping required failures). one_minimal is true if no single item can be removed.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I need to derive a plausible algorithm that matches typical delta debugging but with the described steps. Many chunk reduction algorithms: start with granularity 2, test each chunk. If removal succeeds, keep that removal and continue (restart) with the same granularity? But spec says restart with slightly coarser. Possibly a known algorithm from some paper? Let's try to reverse engineer: In some minimization algorithms, they do coarse-to-fine: start with large chunks, then reduce size after success. But here it's fine-to-coarse. Hmm.\n\nWait: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That means chunk size 2. \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" So we test each consecutive chunk of 2 items; if after removing that chunk the required failure items are still present, we accept that removal (i.e., permanently remove that chunk). \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That could mean after we accept a removal, we increase the chunk size (e.g., from 2 to 3) and then start over from left with that new chunk size. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" - if at current granularity, no chunk removal is acceptable (i.e., all removals would remove a required failure item), then we double the chunk size (e.g., 2->4, 3->6, etc.). \"Stop at singleton granularity.\" Means we stop when chunk size becomes 1. But we start at 2, so we only ever increase chunk size (coarser). We never reach 1. Unless we also have a decrease? Maybe after doubling, if we go beyond the total length or something we stop? No, singleton means 1. So maybe the algorithm actually goes fine to coarse, and once we reach a chunk size that is larger than the number of items, we stop? But then we never get to 1. \n\nPerhaps \"singleton granularity\" means each item individually (chunk size 1). That is the finest. So algorithm starts at coarse (2), then after successful removal, goes finer (coarser? ambiguous). Could be that \"coarser\" is a misnomer and actually means \"finer\". In some contexts, \"coarser\" could mean the granularity number is larger (coarser), but the algorithm they intend is: start with large chunks, after success, try smaller chunks (finer). That would make sense for minimization: you try to remove large blocks first, then refine. Then the phrase \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" would mean increase the chunk size (go coarser) when no removal works. That is typical: if you can't remove any block of current size, you try larger blocks. Then \"stop at singleton granularity\" means stop when chunk size becomes 1 (i.e., you've tried removing individual items). So the algorithm: start with granularity 2 (small chunks), if a removal succeeds, restart with slightly coarser (larger)? That doesn't fit. Let's reverse: start with granularity 2 (small), if no removal succeeds, double to 4, then if still no success, double to 8, etc., until granularity > len(items) maybe stop. But that would never test granularity 1. That seems not.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is: initial granularity = 2. While granularity >= 1: try chunks of size granularity left to right. If a removal succeeds, commit it, then set granularity = granularity // 2? That would make it finer after success. That's typical delta debugging. But spec says \"restart with slightly coarser\" which is opposite. Or maybe \"coarser\" means the chunk size increases, but \"slightly coarser\" means increase by 1, not double. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" is a separate progression. So maybe both happen: after success, we coarsen slightly (add 1 to granularity); after failure of all chunks, we double granularity. That would still lead to ever-increasing granularity. Might stop when granularity > len(items)? But spec says \"stop at singleton granularity\". Singleton is 1, which is smaller than starting 2. So that would never happen.\n\nI suspect there might be a misinterpretation. Let's read the original task description: \"Implement chunk_reduction_schedule through solve(case) for this bounded objective: Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" That's from the candidate_artifact. The fixtures show \"items\" and \"required_failure_items\". So we need to remove items that are not required, keep required ones. But we have to follow the granularity schedule. The spec says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So the algorithm should produce a reduced list, count of attempts (number of chunk removal tests), and whether the final set is one-minimal (i.e., no single item can be removed while keeping required failures). This is reminiscent of the \"Chunk reduction\" algorithm used in some fuzzing/minimization tools.\n\nGiven the lack of clarity, I'll have to make a reasonable interpretation that produces deterministic output for the given fixtures. Since the fixtures are simple (10 items, 2 required), the algorithm might quickly reduce to just the required items. Let's test: items = [\"t0\",\"t1\",...,\"t9\"], required = [\"t0\",\"t6\"]. We start granularity 2. Chunks: indices [0,1] items t0,t1. Remove chunk? Check if required failures remain after removal: after removing t0,t1, remaining items include t6, so yes? But t0 is required, so removing t0 loses a required failure item. So the condition \"required failure items remain\" means all required items must still be present after removal. So removal of a chunk that contains a required item is not allowed. So chunk [0,1] contains t0 -> not allowed. Next chunk [2,3] contains t2,t3 -> allowed because required items are t0,t6 not in chunk. So we can remove [2,3]. After removal, items become [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Then we restart with slightly coarser granularity. Starting from granularity 2, after success, we go to 3 (coarser). Now items length 8. Try chunks of size 3 left to right: [t0,t1,t4] contains t0 -> not allowed. [t1,t4,t5] allowed? It doesn't contain t0 or t6 -> allowed. So remove them. Then items become [t0,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]? Actually after removing [t1,t4,t5], remaining: t0, t6,t7,t8,t9? Wait original after first removal: [t0,t1,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Remove indices 1-3 (t1,t4,t5) yields [t0, t6,t7,t8,t9] (since original positions: 0:t0,1:t1,2:t4,3:t5,4:t6,5:t7,6:t8,7:t9; after removing 1-3, we keep indices 0,4,5,6,7 -> t0,t6,t7,t8,t9). Then restart with slightly coarser granularity: now granularity=4. Items length 5. Chunks of size 4: [t0,t6,t7,t8] contains t0 and t6 -> not allowed. [t6,t7,t8,t9] contains t6 -> not allowed. So no removal succeeds. Then double granularity to 8 (since no removal succeeded). But items length 5, chunk size 8 > length -> maybe skip? The spec says \"stop at singleton granularity\". At this point, granularity 8 is not singleton (1). Actually we should double only if no removal succeeds, but if chunk size > length, we might consider it as trying? Or stop? We need to handle that. Since stop at singleton, we would eventually go to granularity 1? But we are doubling to 8, then next if no success, double to 16, etc. That never reaches 1. So that algorithm doesn't make sense.\n\nMaybe the \"granularity\" refers to the number of chunks, not chunk size. For example, granularity 2 means splitting into 2 chunks. That is typical in delta debugging: number of partitions. Then \"slightly coarser\" means fewer partitions (larger chunks), \"double granularity\" means more partitions (finer). \"Stop at singleton granularity\" means each item is its own chunk (max partitions). That would make sense: start with 2 partitions (two large chunks). If removal of a partition succeeds, restart with slightly coarser (fewer partitions, e.g., 1?) That still odd. Actually, delta debugging often starts with 2 partitions, then if failure is mixed, increases partitions (finer). But here it says \"coarser\" after success, which would mean fewer partitions. And \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means more partitions. That pattern: when a removal works, you become confident and try larger chunks (coarser) to remove more at once; when no removal works, you need finer granularity to isolate. That matches typical optimization: try big chunks first, if that fails, go smaller. So \"granularity\" is the number of chunks (divisions). \"Singleton granularity\" means each item is its own chunk (granularity = len(items)). So start with granularity = 2 (2 chunks). For each chunk, if removing that chunk (i.e., all items in that chunk) still keeps required failures, then accept removal and restart with granularity = 1 (coarser? Actually 1 chunk is coarser than 2). But \"slightly coarser\" suggests decreasing granularity gradually, not jumping to 1. Maybe after success, we decrement granularity by 1 (coarser). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means if no chunk removal works, then we double the number of chunks (finer). That would eventually reach singleton (granularity = len(items)) when we keep doubling. And stop at singleton? That would be the maximum granularity. So algorithm: start with g=2. While g <= len(items): try to remove each chunk (non-overlapping contiguous partitions of roughly equal size). If any chunk removal succeeds, accept that removal (permanently remove that chunk), then set g = g-1 (coarser) and restart. If no removal succeeds for current g, set g = g*2 (finer). Stop when g > len(items)? Or stop at singleton meaning g = len(items) then we stop after testing? Possibly stop when g == len(items) and no removal succeeds? This interpretation seems plausible.\n\nLet's test with fixture: items length 10, required [t0,t6].\nStart g=2 => 2 chunks: chunk0 indices 0-4 (t0..t4), chunk1 indices 5-9 (t5..t9). Remove chunk0: contains t0, so not allowed. Remove chunk1: contains t6? Actually t6 is in chunk1 (indices 5: t5,6:t6,...), so contains t6, not allowed. So no removal succeeds. Then double g to 4 (finer). g=4 => 4 chunks of size 2 or 3? Typically we split into g chunks of roughly equal size. len=10, 4 chunks: sizes could be 3,3,2,2? Let's define: chunk0 indices 0-2 (t0,t1,t2), chunk1 3-5 (t3,t4,t5), chunk2 6-7 (t6,t7), chunk3 8-9 (t8,t9). Remove chunk0: contains t0 => no. chunk1: contains t5 but not t6? No t6, but t5 is not required; required are t0,t6. chunk1 doesn't contain t0 or t6, so removal allowed. So we accept removal of chunk1 (indices 3-5: t3,t4,t5). Remove them. Items become [t0,t1,t2, t6,t7,t8,t9] (7 items). Then restart with g = g-1 = 3 (coarser). Now items length 7, g=3. Partition into 3 chunks: sizes 3,2,2? chunk0: indices 0-2 (t0,t1,t2) contains t0 -> no. chunk1: 3-4 (t6,t7) contains t6 -> no. chunk2: 5-6 (t8,t9) no required -> allowed. So remove chunk2 (t8,t9). Items become [t0,t1,t2,t6,t7] (5 items). Restart with g=2 (coarser). g=2: partition 5 items into 2 chunks: chunk0 indices 0-2 (t0,t1,t2) contains t0->no. chunk1 indices3-4 (t6,t7) contains t6->no. No removal succeeds. Then double g to 4 (finer). g=4: partition 5 items into 4 chunks: sizes 2,1,1,1? chunk0: indices0-1 (t0,t1) contains t0->no. chunk1: indices2 (t2) no required->allowed? Remove chunk1 (t2). But careful: we test each chunk left to right. First chunk0 fails, skip. chunk1: contains only t2, not required, so removal allowed. Accept. Items become [t0,t1,t6,t7] (4 items). Restart with g=3 (coarser). g=3: partition 4 items: chunk0 indices0-1 (t0,t1) contains t0->no. chunk1 index2 (t6) contains t6->no. chunk2 index3 (t7) not required->allowed. Remove chunk2 (t7). Items: [t0,t1,t6] (3 items). Restart with g=2. g=2: partition 3 items: chunk0 indices0-1 (t0,t1) contains t0->no. chunk1 index2 (t6) contains t6->no. No removal. Double to g=4. g=4: but items length 3, we cannot make 4 chunks; we might cap at 3? The algorithm should stop? Usually delta debugging stops when granularity exceeds length. Here \"singleton granularity\" might mean each item is its own chunk (granularity = len). So we can go to g=3 (since len=3