3, fails. chunk2 -> attempt 14, succeeds. accepted. items updated. restart g=2.\nItems length 3.\ng=2: test chunk0 -> attempt 15, fails. chunk1 -> attempt 16, fails. no success, double g -> g=4.\nItems length 3.\ng=4: Since g > len(items)? Actually len=3, g=4 >3. Should we attempt partitions? Typically we cap g to len(items). In delta debugging, when g > n, we set g = n (singleton). So we should set g = min(g, len(items)). So g becomes 3. Then test each singleton: attempt 17, test chunk0 (t0) -> fails; attempt 18, chunk1 (t1) -> succeeds, accepted. items updated to [t0,t6]. restart g=2.\nItems length 2.\ng=2: test chunk0 (t0) -> attempt 19, fails; chunk1 (t6) -> attempt 20, fails. no success, double g -> g=4. Since g=4 > len=2, we set g=2 (singleton) again? Actually we should stop when no removal succeeded and g is already at singleton? In our loop condition, we stop when g > len and we have already tested singleton? We need to define precise termination.\n\nAlternative approach: We keep iterating until g > len(items) and no removal succeeded at previous step? The spec says \"stop at singleton granularity\". That suggests that the algorithm terminates when we reach granularity = 1 (singleton). But here singleton means each item as chunk. So we should continue until we have tested granularity = len (each item) and no removal succeeded. Then we stop. So after we doubled to g=4, but len=2, we should set g = len (2) which is already tested? Actually we just tested g=2 and no success. So we are already at singleton. Then the algorithm would try to double to 4, but since 4 > len, we stop. So final state is after testing g=2 with no success. So no further removals. So final items [t0,t6].\n\nAttempts total: we counted up to attempt 20. Let's recount: \n- Attempt 1 (g=2 chunk0), 2 (chunk1) - no success, double.\n- Attempt 3 (g=4 chunk0), 4 (chunk1 success) - accepted.\n- Attempt 5 (g=3 chunk0), 6 (chunk1), 7 (chunk2 success) - accepted.\n- Attempt 8 (g=2 chunk0), 9 (chunk1) - no success, double.\n- Attempt 10 (g=4 chunk0), 11 (chunk1 success) - accepted.\n- Attempt 12 (g=3 chunk0), 13 (chunk1), 14 (chunk2 success) - accepted.\n- Attempt 15 (g=2 chunk0), 16 (chunk1) - no success, double.\n- Attempt 17 (g=3?) Actually after no success at g=2, double to g=4, but len=3, so we should attempt g=3 (since cap at len). So attempt 17 (g=3 chunk0 t0) fail, 18 (g=3 chunk1 t1) success - accepted.\n- Items become 2.\n- Attempt 19 (g=2 chunk0 t0) fail, 20 (g=2 chunk1 t6) fail - no success. Then double to g=4 > len=2, stop. So total attempts = 20.\n\nThus reduced_items = [\"t0\",\"t6\"], attempts=20, one_minimal=True.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns exactly that.\n\nWe need to be careful: The algorithm must be deterministic and respect the specific steps: start granularity 2, try chunks left to right, accept removal only if required failure items remain, restart after accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity (decrease g by 1? or maintain same? \"slightly coarser\" suggests increase chunk size = decrease g). Double granularity when no removal succeeds. Stop at singleton granularity.\n\nWe'll implement with g starting at 2, and a loop that continues until we have reached and tested singleton and no more progress. Use a while True loop. Keep a variable \"restart\" flag to indicate we need to reset chunk scanning. After acceptance, set g = max(1, g-1) (coarser) and restart scanning from left. If no removal in a full pass at current g, set g = g*2 (but cap at len(items) because we cannot have more partitions than items). If after update g > len(items), we stop. But we also need to handle the case where g becomes 1 (which would be finer than 2? Actually g=1 is coarser than 2? In terms of number of partitions, g=1 means single chunk including all items. That would be coarser. But we start at g=2 and only ever increase g via doubling, so we never go to g=1. But after success, we decrease g by 1, which could eventually go to 1. That's fine. But \"singleton granularity\" likely means g == len(items) (each item separate). So we should stop when we have tested g == len(items) (or when g > len(items) after testing). So we should allow g to be up to len(items). When we double and it exceeds len, we set g = len(items) (if not already tested) and test that. After that, if no removal, stop.\n\nAlso need to handle case where required_failure_items might be empty? Possibly, but not in fixtures.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Input: case is dict with 'items' (list of strings) and 'required_failure_items' (list of strings). We'll ignore '_case_nonce'.\n- We'll operate on a mutable list copy.\n- Function to test removal of a chunk: Given list of items, start index and end index (exclusive), temporarily remove slice, check if all required items are still present. Use set for quick membership.\n- The chunk partitions: given current g and item list length n, we need to divide into g non-overlapping contiguous chunks that cover all items. They should be as equal as possible. For simplicity, we can compute chunk sizes: base = n // g, remainder = n % g. Then for i from 0 to g-1, start = i*base + min(i, remainder), end = start + base + (1 if i < remainder else 0). This gives exactly g chunks covering all items.\n- We'll iterate i from 0 to g-1 in order.\n- For each chunk, we test removal. If removal accepted, we modify the list (remove that chunk), record attempt increment, then break out of chunk loop, set g = max(1, g-1) (coarser), and restart scanning (i.e., reset to first chunk of new g on the new list).\n- If we finish the loop without any acceptance, we increment attempts for each test (each chunk tested). Then set g = g*2. If g > len(items) and g != len(items?), we set g = len(items) if not already tested? Better to maintain a set of tested g values? But simple approach: if after doubling g > len(items), set g = len(items). But we must avoid infinite loops: if g was already len(items) and no removal, 